"Kent Paul Dolan" <xanthian@well.com> writes:
> I don't know _how_ it is accomplished in a
> multi-player installation of NetHack, but I'm
> guessing that it is, that more than just one bones
> file per level can exist, so you are bringing the
> individual installation into slow parity to a large
> multi-player installation.

Nope. One file per level; the bones code doesn't care whether you're
on a single-player or multi-player system.

> Even I'm wrong, and  only one bones file can exist
> per level, on a large installation either, most
> levels would have bones files "most of the time",
> I'd think, though that might depend on whether the
> chance of dropping a bones file equals the chance of
> picking up an existing bones file.

The chances are different; the chance of loading is a flat 1/3, while
that of saving increases with depth. The greater dependence, though,
is on the relative skills of the players, in so far as it affects
which levels they'll be reaching and which they'll be dying on. Eva
Myers has written an analysis of bones populations on single- and
multi-player systems which makes interesting reading:

http://www.statslab.cam.ac.uk/~eva/nethack/iaq.html#bones

> Which reminds me of a problem that's been puzzling
> me; if a bones is dropped on normal level four, that
> doesn't have a Mines Town entrance, but a subsequent
> game reaches level four without a Mines Town
> entrance being created, what happens in the game
> mechanics at level four?
> 
> 1) No possibility to use a bones file is considered.

I think you may have a slight misconception as to the way the dungeon
layout is determined; the game doesn't check on creating each level
"hmm, should I branch here?", instead predetermining at game-start
that the Mines branch will happen on level 4, the Quest on 14, etc.
(The exception is the Ludios portal, which _is_ created on the fly;
hence the possibility of Ludios being unreachable if its portal never
happens to be generated.) Thus, on entering a level on which a branch
is due to be created, bones aren't checked for.

> The opposite problem exists if a DLVL 4 bones file _does_ contain a
> Mines Town entrance.

Branching levels can't be left as bones.

